Moth favours syntax that lets you answer a question where it comes up. To see what a call can change, look at the parameter and the argument.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    increment |count ~I32| {
        ~count += 1
    }

    total ~= 2
    increment(~total)
]

The parameter asks for mutable access. The caller grants it by writing `~total`. After the call, `total` holds 3. You can see which existing value the function may change before you read its body.

Moth also reuses the same rules where two operations take arguments. A named struct constructor and an ordinary function call both accept positional arguments followed by named arguments. Once you know how to supply arguments to one, you can read the other. See @../language-overview/ (Language basics) for an example of each.

### Write against a package contract

A program uses Moth and the packages its builder provides. The compiler and builder turn that program into JavaScript, Wasm or another artefact. The same source rules apply on each target.

### Directives stay constrained

Compiler-known directives may carry language or structural meaning. Arbitrary
macros and builder AST transformations do not. Ordinary `$config` values and
static `if` cannot remove declarations or dependencies.

Future `$feature` is the separate structural mechanism. It selects source
structure before graph publication from bootstrap-known data and the selected
builder identity. The compiler must distinguish a known but disabled feature from an unknown
name so a typo cannot silently disable code. The full rules for predicates,
nesting and configuration declarations remain open.

### Target and representation boundaries

Source cannot query a physical target, OS, architecture or backend. Builder
identity is not physical target identity. Excluded regions still need a sound
structural boundary before unknown syntax can be skipped.

The proposed `$layout` directive would constrain how a type is stored while preserving its identity. The proposed `$test` root purpose also remains deferred. Today `$export` marks public declarations and re-exports on a facade. Project configuration uses a closed named record. Custom metadata remains an open question.
